The Python statement if __name__ == "__main__": is a common idiom used to control the execution of code when a file is run directly versus when it's imported as a module.

What It Means
•	Every Python file has a special built-in variable called __name__.
•	If the file is being run directly (e.g., python my_script.py), then __name__ is set to "__main__".
•	If the file is being imported into another script, then __name__ is set to the module's name.
Example
def greet():
    print("Hello from greet()!")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    greet()

•	If you run this file directly, it prints: Hello from greet()!
•	If you import this file into another script, nothing happens unless you explicitly call greet().
 Why Use It?
•	Keeps your code modular and reusable.
•	Prevents certain code from running during import.
•	Useful for testing or running scripts independently.

